  "numerical_failure": {
    "category": "correctness",
    "affected_api": null,
    "observed_behavior": "AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'T' when evaluating `.value` for `cp.lambda_max(X)`, `cp.lambda_sum_largest(X, 2)`, `cp.log_det(X)`, or `cp.tr_inv(X)` where `X` is a symmetric variable whose `.value` is `None` and a symmetry/Hermitian check runs before the None check.",
    "expected_behavior": "Return `None` (avoid the AttributeError) when the variable values are `None`, by checking `self.args[0].value is None` before running the Hermitian/symmetry `np.allclose(..., ...T.conj())` check.",
    "manifestation": "exception",
    "trigger_conditions": [
      "Calling `.value` on `cp.lambda_max(X)`, `cp.lambda_sum_largest(X, 2)`, `cp.log_det(X)`, or `cp.tr_inv(X)` when variable `X` value is `None`",
      "Execution order bug: the symmetry check involving `self.args[0].value.T.conj()` runs before the None check"
    ],
    "input_output_examples": [
      {
        "input": "import cvxpy as cp\nX = cp.Variable((3, 3), symmetric=True)\ncp.lambda_max(X).value",
        "buggy_output": "AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'T'",
        "expected_output": null,
        "value_kind": "exception",
        "source": "issue comment 3"
      },
      {
        "input": "import cvxpy as cp\nX = cp.Variable((3, 3), symmetric=True)\ncp.lambda_sum_largest(X, 2).value",
        "buggy_output": "AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'T'",
        "expected_output": null,
        "value_kind": "exception",
        "source": "issue comment 3"
      },
      {
        "input": "import cvxpy as cp\nX = cp.Variable((3, 3), symmetric=True)\ncp.log_det(X).value",
        "buggy_output": "AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'T'",
        "expected_output": null,
        "value_kind": "exception",
        "source": "issue comment 3"
      },
      {
        "input": "import cvxpy as cp\nX = cp.Variable((3, 3), symmetric=True)\ncp.tr_inv(X).value",
        "buggy_output": "AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'T'",
        "expected_output": null,
        "value_kind": "exception",
        "source": "issue comment 3"
      }
    ],
    "oracle_clues": [
      "PR#3180: `lambda_max.value` (and `lambda_sum_largest`, `tr_inv`, `log_det`): crashed with `AttributeError` when variable values are None because a `np.allclose` symmetry check ran before the None check; it was reordered.",
      "PR#3180 issue reproducer shows the missing check: `if any(p.value is None for p in self.parameters()): return None` then `if self.args[0].value is None: return None` (missing in the PR) before `np.allclose(self.args[0].value, self.args[0].value.T.conj())`.",
      "The `lambda_sum_largest` implementation diff shows the fix ordering: `if self.args[0].value is None: return None` is evaluated before the `np.allclose(...value.T.conj())` symmetry check."
    ]
  },
  "numerical_characteristics": {
    "dtype": null,
    "precision": null,
    "magnitude": null,
    "tolerance": null,
    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "PR#3180: `log_det.sign_from_args` incorrectly claimed log(det(X)) is always nonneg; it can be negative (example given: `log(det(0.5*I)) < 0`). Changed to `(False, False)`.",
      "PR#3180: `dist_ratio` sublevel set used `(1-tsq**2)` i.e. `1-t^4` instead of `(1-tsq)` i.e. `1-t^2`.",
      "PR#3180: `PSD.residual` used `.T` instead of `.H`, giving wrong results for complex Hermitian matrices (mathematical property: complex Hermitian conjugate-transpose)."
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "The only concrete numerical example values in the passages are for the sign issue (`0.5*I` leading to negative log(det)); if other reproductions rely on matrix sizes/values not explicitly provided here, they may not match the described violation.",
      "The Hermitian/symmetry check in the provided reproducer raises based on whether `np.allclose(self.args[0].value, self.args[0].value.T.conj())` holds; using non-Hermitian numerical matrices will trigger a ValueError path rather than demonstrating the intended numerical-correctness issue.",
      "Complex-vs-real behavior matters for the `.T` vs `.H` change; using purely real matrices would be a weaker test for that specific violation."
    ]
  },
